  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 AM EDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nAs a mid level trough digs across the Central US, an upper low gets \nshoved northeastward pushing a surface low into southeast \nCanada/Northeast US. At the same time, a 1040 mb surface high slides \nsoutheastward into the Central CONUS. The presence of this strong \nsurface high often indicates the arrival of a cooler airmass and \nthat is the case this time. Given our departure of above normal \ntemperatures and the arrival of this cool airmass, we'll have some \nhazards to contend with today and this weekend. First, yesterday's \ncold front shifts eastward and the vigorous vort max associated with \nthe mid level trough dives through Lk MI late this afternoon into \nthis evening. Given the warm lake and the 850 mb temps dipping to \naround or below 0C, lake enhancement is expected to cause rain.\nA surface trough is expected to swing through the southeast \narea of Lk MI around 18z with ample low level moisture, low \nlevel wind convergence, and building lake enhanced instability. \nGiven the movement of this trough and swiveling trajectories \nfrom northwest to north, the area with the best chance to see \n\"higher\" QPF is southern Berrien into LaPorte where 0.25 to 0.5\"\nis forecast, tapering off the farther away from the lake you \nare. The heaviest of the rain appears to get into Berrien county\nmidday (15 or 16z) and continues swiveling southward through \nthe afternoon and into the evening. The other hazard is wind \ngusts, which look to get underway along lk MI- adjacent \nlocations around 19 or 20z before pulling away around 3z. Given \nthe surface trough, there could be some isentropic downglide \nenough to work an isolated 45 kt gust down to the surface. \nOtherwise, it appears that frequent gusts between 30 and 40 mph \nwill be possible during that time as low level lapse rates \nbecome conducive. The strongest of the gusts are adjacent to the\nlake, tapering off the farther away you get. This is why the \nwind advisory was hoisted for Lk MI- adjacent locales. \n\nThe mention of the cold air also brings to mind the chance for \nfrost. Especially with overnight lows dropping into the 30s from \nfrom tonight/Sat AM to Sunday night/Mon AM and potentially \nagain Tue AM. The main issue with Friday night/Sat AM is the \ngradient which could keep winds strong enough to eliminate \nformation of frost. A lesser possibility is lake clouds hang on,\nbut would probably lean towards subsidence on the eastern side \nof the lake clouds removing them. Clouds appear to already be \ndissipating on the NAM by around 6 to 9z for maybe all of our \ncounties (lowest confidence on LaPorte to White county line).\nThis would give areas along or about IN-15 north of US-24 on \ninto MI the best chance to see frost tonight. Surface high \npressure continues to poke into the area Saturday morning with \nthe stronger gradient lurking to our north. So, cloud cover \nappears less of factor. It's often that as return flow begins to\noccur that we get our best frost chances as winds slacken, but \nthe boundary layer is able to get back to what it was the \nprevious night or potentially colder. So, Saturday night has the\nbest chance for frost. There is also the possibility of a \nfreeze primarily along and south of US-24 Sun AM with a couple \nhours of temps right around there then.\n\nSurface high pressure then becomes stagnant across the eastern CONUS \nas blocking builds across the Atlantic. The NAO/AO both drop \nnegative during this time. The upper jet also retreats back up into \nCanada. A slow moving trough moves into the western CONUS, but takes \nits time arriving into the area potentially continuing to provide \ndry weather into next weekend. Temperatures trend warmer with highs \nreturning to the 70s during the next work week and lows warming \nslowly through the 40s.",
